00:10:41,849 --> 00:10:43,809
[laughter]

253
00:10:43,893 --> 00:10:46,145
Not a soldier.
He was there for a bachelor party.

254
00:10:46,646 --> 00:10:48,648
[laughter]

255
00:10:50,024 --> 00:10:52,109
It was a fentanyl overdose.

256
00:10:52,193 --> 00:10:54,195
[laughter]

257
00:10:57,531 --> 00:10:59,408
I did have a really scary thought
the other day.

258
00:10:59,492 --> 00:11:02,703
Imagine having a kid
if you didn't want one.

259
00:11:03,412 --> 00:11:05,206
-[exhales slowly]
-[laughter]

260
00:11:05,289 --> 00:11:06,916
Imagine having a kid if you didn't want…

261
00:11:06,999 --> 00:11:09,502
Imagine having a lamp
if you didn't want one.

262
00:11:09,585 --> 00:11:10,586
[laughter]

263
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,130
Like a lamp shows up at your house
you didn't order.

264
00:11:13,214 --> 00:11:15,883
You're just like, "What the fuck
am I gonna do with this lamp?"

265
00:11:16,384 --> 00:11:19,011
Then you have to keep it in your house
for 18 years.

266
00:11:20,304 --> 00:11:22,848
I have my hands full right now.
I adopted a puppy.

267
00:11:22,932 --> 00:11:24,058
[cheering]

268
00:11:25,476 --> 00:11:26,894
Her name is Pinky.

269
00:11:26,977 --> 00:11:28,437
She's a… Yeah.

270
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:29,563
[cheering]

271
00:11:29,647 --> 00:11:31,982
She's a chihuahua from Tijuana.

272
00:11:32,566 --> 00:11:35,986
She crossed the border to take the job
from an American chihuahua.

273
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:37,154
[laughter]

274
00:11:37,238 --> 00:11:39,031
She just has better work ethic.

275
00:11:39,115 --> 00:11:40,658
[laughter]

276
00:11:40,741 --> 00:11:42,284
She is a dog and an immigrant.

277
00:11:42,368 --> 00:11:44,995
We keep her away from Kristi Noem
for two reasons.

278
00:11:45,079 --> 00:11:46,539
[laughter]

279
00:11:46,622 --> 00:11:48,624
[applause]

280
00:11:49,125 --> 00:11:50,126
Thank you.

281
00:11:52,169 --> 00:11:54,839
I found a neurodivergent dog trainer
for Pinky.

282
00:11:55,339 --> 00:11:56,298
[laughter]

283
00:11:56,382 --> 00:11:59,844
I looked for one specifically 'cause
I didn't want it to be a hobby for them.

284
00:11:59,927 --> 00:12:01,929
[laughter]

285
00:12:03,472 --> 00:12:05,683
I wanted it to be an obsession.

286
00:12:07,017 --> 00:12:08,769
So we're teaching her to bite.

287
00:12:08,853 --> 00:12:10,813
[laughter]

288
00:12:10,896 --> 00:12:12,148
No, I need her to be a good dog.

289
00:12:12,231 --> 00:12:15,025
I had a bad dog once before
and I cannot go through that again.

290
00:12:15,109 --> 00:12:16,944
Our family dog growing up,
her name was Bailey.

291
00:12:17,027 --> 00:12:18,320
She was a Jack Russell terrier

292
00:12:18,404 --> 00:12:20,364
and she held our family hostage.

293
00:12:20,448 --> 00:12:21,407
[laughter]

294
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:23,659
She was a domestic terrorist.

295
00:12:23,743 --> 00:12:25,703
I think it was the Irish in her.

296
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:27,747
[laughter]

297
00:12:29,790 --> 00:12:31,751
I'm not just saying
she would bark at the vacuum.

298
00:12:31,834 --> 00:12:35,755
Bailey would actually bite car tires
as people were pulling out of our driveway

299
00:12:36,255 --> 00:12:37,798
and pop holes in them.

300
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:41,010
She was responsible for 17 flat tires

301
00:12:41,719 --> 00:12:43,137
and three car bombs.

302
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:44,972
[laughter]

303
00:12:45,055 --> 00:12:47,641
So I really need Pinky to be a good dog.
She's doing pretty well.

304
00:12:47,725 --> 00:12:50,144
She's been pulling on a leash a bit
when we're out on walks.

305
00:12:50,227 --> 00:12:51,979
So I called the trainer.
"What should I do?"

306
00:12:52,062 --> 00:12:54,315
And she said,
"Well, some people put meat on a stick

307
00:12:54,398 --> 00:12:57,610
and they take it on the walks with them
to keep the dog engaged."

308
00:12:57,693 --> 00:12:58,903
[laughter]

309
00:12:58,986 --> 00:13:01,113
I was like, "Okay, so what should <i>I </i>do?"

310
00:13:01,197 --> 00:13:03,199
[laughter]

311
00:13:03,282 --> 00:13:05,868
"I can't walk around Brooklyn
with meat on a stick."

312
00:13:05,951 --> 00:13:07,369
[laughter]

313
00:13:07,453 --> 00:13:08,871
"I'm in a TV show."

314
00:13:08,954 --> 00:13:11,332
[laughter]

315
00:13:15,669 --> 00:13:19,006
So I have to send her away
to a week-long intensive training camp.

316
00:13:19,089 --> 00:13:21,509
I was filling out
the 90-minute onboarding survey

317
00:13:21,592 --> 00:13:23,594
where you answer questions about your dog.

318
00:13:23,677 --> 00:13:26,013
One of the questions was
"Has your dog ever bitten a human?"

319
00:13:26,096 --> 00:13:28,432
"If so, please rate the severity
from one to seven."

320
00:13:28,516 --> 00:13:30,976
So one was it left a mark,
but it didn't break the skin.

321
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:33,062
Seven was the victim died.

322
00:13:33,145 --> 00:13:34,230
[laughter]

323
00:13:36,482 --> 00:13:39,276
I was like,
"Are you guys training killer dogs here?"

324
00:13:40,069 --> 00:13:43,072
But I caught myself being judgmental
because crime is a social issue.

325
00:13:43,572 --> 00:13:45,157
[laughter]

326
00:13:45,241 --> 00:13:49,036
And I'm raising Pinky
as an atheist socialist feminist.

327
00:13:49,119 --> 00:13:51,664
[cheering and applause]

328
00:13:51,747 --> 00:13:53,666
Sometimes I feel like I'm failing her.

329
00:13:54,250 --> 00:13:57,378
'Cause your dog is a reflection of you,
and I'm really hard on myself.

330
00:13:57,461 --> 00:13:58,504
We had a very tough moment.

331
00:13:58,587 --> 00:14:01,590
We were at John F. Kennedy International
Airport. We were going on vacation.

332
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:02,800
It was six in the morning

333
00:14:02,883 --> 00:14:06,554
and Pinky decided to take a shit
in the automated revolving door.

334
00:14:06,637 --> 00:14:07,638
[laughter]

335
00:14:09,682 --> 00:14:12,393
What do I mean by automated?
It just keeps going.

336
00:14:13,811 --> 00:14:16,272
It was so early.
I was like, "She doesn't have to go yet."

337
00:14:16,355 --> 00:14:19,608
As soon as we stepped inside
this revolving door, she took a shit.

338
00:14:20,109 --> 00:14:22,069
It happened so fast.
There was nothing I could do.

339
00:14:22,152 --> 00:14:24,905
When we walked out, I was panicking.
I'm like, "I need to tell someone."

340
00:14:24,989 --> 00:14:28,367
"If I just leave this here, it will
eat away at me for the rest of my life."

341
00:14:28,450 --> 00:14:31,328
And it's like, who do you tell
something like that to at the airport?

342
00:14:31,954 --> 00:14:34,915
Everyone that works there now
just pushes wheelchairs.

343
00:14:34,999 --> 00:14:36,292
[laughter]

344
00:14:36,375 --> 00:14:39,503
Can't tell the ticketing desk.
They're so mean, they'll scream at me.

345
00:14:40,004 --> 00:14:42,548
This isn't Canada, okay? They have guns.

346
00:14:42,631 --> 00:14:43,966
[laughter]

347
00:14:44,049 --> 00:14:47,177
So I thought the only appropriate thing
to do after what just happened,

348
00:14:47,261 --> 00:14:49,346
I'm gonna get on the plane
and I'm gonna blow it up.

349
00:14:49,889 --> 00:14:51,891
[laughter]

350
00:14:53,225 --> 00:14:55,644
'Cause I'll never be allowed back at JFK.

351
00:14:55,728 --> 00:14:56,770
[laughter]

352
00:14:56,854 --> 00:14:59,690
So I went through security
thinking that's what I was gonna do.

353
00:14:59,773 --> 00:15:01,191
[laughter]

354
00:15:01,275 --> 00:15:04,069
And then I saw these two women
at the help desk at Terminal 4.

355
00:15:04,153 --> 00:15:05,279
They had very kind faces.

356
00:15:05,362 --> 00:15:09,325
I just walked up to them and I said,
"Um, something really bad just happened."

357
00:15:09,408 --> 00:15:10,910
[laughter]

358
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:11,827
They said, "What?"

359
00:15:11,911 --> 00:15:14,121
I said, "My dog took a shit
in the revolving door."

360
00:15:14,204 --> 00:15:16,999
Keep in mind, every second
I'm waiting to tell someone about this,

361
00:15:17,082 --> 00:15:19,752
the shit's just going
around and around and around.

362
00:15:19,835 --> 00:15:21,503
[laughter]

363
00:15:21,587 --> 00:15:24,506
So I told them my dog took a shit
in the automated revolving door.

364
00:15:24,590 --> 00:15:28,177
But when she put her hand on my hand,
she said, "It's going to be okay."

365
00:15:28,260 --> 00:15:29,678
[laughter]

366
00:15:29,762 --> 00:15:30,846
We had a laugh about it.

367
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:33,682
She said, "We'll call upstairs,
they'll stop the door, they'll clean it,

368
00:15:33,766 --> 00:15:35,976
and also, this has never happened before."

369
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:37,353
[laughter]

370
00:15:37,436 --> 00:15:39,563
And then I was feeling a little proud.

371
00:15:40,439 --> 00:15:45,194
And I looked down at Pinky and I said,
"Well-behaved women rarely make history."

372
00:15:45,277 --> 00:15:47,279
[laughter and applause]

373
00:15:50,616 --> 00:15:52,034
Pinky is very inspiring to me.

374
00:15:52,117 --> 00:15:54,411
She's actually inspired me
to write a movie.

375
00:15:54,495 --> 00:15:56,205
It's a body swap comedy.

376
00:15:56,288 --> 00:15:57,122
[laughter]

377
00:15:57,206 --> 00:16:00,793
And it's about a girl
whose dog swaps bodies with her boyfriend.

378
00:16:00,876 --> 00:16:03,170
And not that much changes.

379
00:16:03,253 --> 00:16:04,338
[laughter]

380
00:16:04,421 --> 00:16:06,590
And in fact,
their relationship gets better.

381
00:16:06,674 --> 00:16:07,800
[laughter]

382
00:16:07,883 --> 00:16:11,053
The dog finally teaches the woman
what unconditional love is.

383
00:16:11,136 --> 00:16:12,805
Then the boyfriend
kind of likes being a dog

384
00:16:12,888 --> 00:16:15,557
'cause then he gets to contribute
to the household even less.

385
00:16:16,475 --> 00:16:18,519
So they decide not to swap back.

386
00:16:18,602 --> 00:16:20,020
[laughter]

387
00:16:20,104 --> 00:16:21,647
And the movie's called <i>Stay.</i>

388
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:23,732
[laughter]

389
00:16:24,900 --> 00:16:26,443
But I'm gonna set it in 2019.

390
00:16:26,527 --> 00:16:29,154
Everything I write from now on
I'm just gonna set in 2019,

391
00:16:29,655 --> 00:16:31,949
so I don't have to address Ozempic.

392
00:16:32,032 --> 00:16:34,034
[laughter]

393
00:16:34,702 --> 00:16:36,453
Ozempic really brings out the worst in me.

394
00:16:36,537 --> 00:16:39,790
Of course I'm happy it's available
for people who need it as medicine.

395
00:16:39,873 --> 00:16:43,419
My dad was a doctor. I love medicine.
I support pharma, big and small.

396
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:46,588
But as someone
that had an eating disorder,

397
00:16:46,672 --> 00:16:49,842
I don't think it's fair that rich people
get the benefits of a mental illness

398
00:16:49,925 --> 00:16:51,552
without actually having it.

399
00:16:51,635 --> 00:16:53,012
[laughter]

400
00:16:53,095 --> 00:16:56,849
No one wants to get off their ass
and have an eating disorder anymore.

401
00:16:56,932 --> 00:16:58,934
[laughter]

402
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:05,357
So imagine if I posted that joke online.

403
00:17:05,441 --> 00:17:07,443
[laughter]

404
00:17:08,819 --> 00:17:10,821
Someone discovered me through that joke.

405
00:17:10,904 --> 00:17:11,947
[laughter]

406
00:17:12,698 --> 00:17:15,284
And I definitely wouldn't recommend
getting an eating disorder.

407
00:17:15,367 --> 00:17:18,037
It's not worth it.
Like, you save a lot of money.

408
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:19,621
[laughter]

409
00:17:19,705 --> 00:17:21,582
But you have all the problems
you had before,

410
00:17:21,665 --> 00:17:23,542
then on top of that, you're hungry.

411
00:17:24,251 --> 00:17:26,795
I know that's resonating
with, like, four girls in here.

412
00:17:26,879 --> 00:17:28,172
-[laughter]
-[woman] Whoo!

413
00:17:28,255 --> 00:17:29,548
And hey, maybe one guy.

414
00:17:30,049 --> 00:17:32,051
[laughter]

415
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:35,179
It's 2026. Guys can have 'em now.

416
00:17:36,346 --> 00:17:38,682
I think it's called intermittent fasting.

417
00:17:38,766 --> 00:17:40,768
[laughter and applause]

418
00:17:44,188 --> 00:17:46,774
I'm a comedian, but I give back.
I'm also an actor.

419
00:17:48,192 --> 00:17:50,778
And with acting, it's a lot
of auditioning. It's a lot of rejection.

420
00:17:50,861 --> 00:17:52,196
That can affect your self-esteem.

421
00:17:52,279 --> 00:17:53,781
Sometimes you get an audition

422
00:17:53,864 --> 00:17:55,866
and you're like,
"I hope I'm not right for that."

423
00:17:56,700 --> 00:17:57,951
They send you a description.

424
00:17:58,035 --> 00:18:00,287
I got an audition once.
The character's name was Sally.

425
00:18:00,370 --> 00:18:05,000
The description said, "Sexy but thin
trailer park, meth addict type…"

426
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:06,418
[laughter]

427
00:18:06,502 --> 00:18:08,754
"…who's seen better days."

428
00:18:08,837 --> 00:18:09,963
[laughter]

429
00:18:10,798 --> 00:18:13,175
Which means that casting saw my headshot,

430
00:18:14,051 --> 00:18:16,220
and they were like,
"This could be our girl."

431
00:18:16,303 --> 00:18:17,387
[laughter]

432
00:18:17,971 --> 00:18:20,474
Trailer park, meth addict type
who's seen better days.

433
00:18:20,557 --> 00:18:23,268
It was actually pretty tame
for a Ryan Murphy project.

434
00:18:23,352 --> 00:18:24,978
[laughter]

435
00:18:25,062 --> 00:18:27,314
I was relieved I did not book that one.

436
00:18:27,397 --> 00:18:30,192
The show that I'm in, <i>Overcompensating,</i>
is about college students,

437
00:18:30,275 --> 00:18:32,569
which is much more my speed at 34.

438
00:18:33,904 --> 00:18:37,116
We filmed a few days on a college campus,
which brought back a lot of memories.

439
00:18:37,199 --> 00:18:39,409
So, who here went to college?
Clap if you went to college.

440
00:18:39,493 --> 00:18:41,495
[applause]

441
00:18:42,454 --> 00:18:44,081
If you went in the US or Canada,

442
00:18:44,164 --> 00:18:48,043
you might remember, on campus, they had
these emergency phones every 200 feet.

443
00:18:48,127 --> 00:18:51,505
These giant red poles with a blue light
on top of them, and they were phones.

444
00:18:51,588 --> 00:18:54,174
When I got to college,
it was explained that those were installed

445
00:18:54,258 --> 00:18:57,052
to help combat sexual assault on campus.

446
00:18:57,553 --> 00:18:59,221
So to me, that just shows

447
00:18:59,304 --> 00:19:03,058
a fundamental misunderstanding
of how sexual assault works.

448
00:19:03,142 --> 00:19:04,226
[laughter]

449
00:19:04,309 --> 00:19:06,603
They don't let you make a phone call.

450
00:19:07,521 --> 00:19:09,523
[laughter]

451
00:19:13,861 --> 00:19:16,280
Did you guys think
they were letting us make a phone call?

452
00:19:16,363 --> 00:19:17,865
[laughter]

453
00:19:17,948 --> 00:19:20,159
I would have loved
to be in that brainstorm.

454
00:19:21,034 --> 00:19:23,537
Trying to come up with ways
to combat sexual assault on campus,

455
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:25,330
there's one woman in the room
and she's like,

456
00:19:25,414 --> 00:19:27,833
"We could just expel students
who assault other students."

457
00:19:27,916 --> 00:19:32,087
Then one of the guys, he's a lot louder,
and he's like, "Those are my friends."

458
00:19:32,171 --> 00:19:33,463
[laughter]

459
00:19:33,547 --> 00:19:36,216
Then another guy's like,
"What if we had more phones?"

460
00:19:37,092 --> 00:19:39,428
"Maybe it's just, like, a lack of phones."

461
00:19:39,511 --> 00:19:41,597
[laughter]

462
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:43,557
Both of those men are in Congress now.

463
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:45,184
[laughter]

464
00:19:45,267 --> 00:19:46,435
Democrats.

465
00:19:46,518 --> 00:19:47,936
[laughter]

466
00:19:48,645 --> 00:19:50,647
[applause]

467
00:19:52,608 --> 00:19:54,193
I don't hate straight guys.

468
00:19:54,276 --> 00:19:56,528
I don't, despite their best efforts.

469
00:19:56,612 --> 00:19:57,529
[laughter]

470
00:19:57,613 --> 00:20:01,200
I'm actually dating one right now.
I'm in a relationship. Yeah.

471
00:20:01,283 --> 00:20:02,618
[cheering]

472
00:20:02,701 --> 00:20:04,244
I'm in an open relationship.

473
00:20:04,328 --> 00:20:07,080
My boyfriend and I are very open.
We broke up.

474
00:20:07,164 --> 00:20:09,249
[laughter]

475
00:20:09,333 --> 00:20:11,293
That's about as open as it gets.

476
00:20:11,919 --> 00:20:14,379
And we did long distance
New York to London for five years

477
00:20:14,463 --> 00:20:15,464
'cause he's British.

478
00:20:15,547 --> 00:20:17,591
So the day we broke up,
I walked out of my apartment,

479
00:20:17,674 --> 00:20:20,552
I went over to the East River
and I just dumped a teabag in there.

480
00:20:20,636 --> 00:20:22,179
[laughter]

481
00:20:22,262 --> 00:20:23,931
It felt like the right thing to do.

482
00:20:25,265 --> 00:20:28,644
And being single when you're bisexual,
it's like, "Really? No one?"

483
00:20:28,727 --> 00:20:30,729
[laughter]

484
00:20:31,939 --> 00:20:33,607
'Cause it's out of everyone.

485
00:20:33,690 --> 00:20:34,816
[laughter]

486
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:36,318
And it's still no one.

487
00:20:36,401 --> 00:20:37,486
[laughter]

488
00:20:37,569 --> 00:20:38,904
But it feels different this time

489
00:20:38,987 --> 00:20:41,907
'cause I have zero interest
in having casual sex with strangers.

490
00:20:41,990 --> 00:20:43,450
I'm just not casual sex girl,

491
00:20:43,533 --> 00:20:46,995
and it took me 34 years on this Earth
to admit that about myself.

492
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:50,082
I fall in love with almost every
single person I've ever had sex with.

493
00:20:50,165 --> 00:20:53,710
I put my entire self-worth
and my life's happiness in their hands.

494
00:20:53,794 --> 00:20:57,714
When they don't respond to a text from me,
I go into a state of deep psychosis.

495
00:20:57,798 --> 00:20:59,341
[laughter]

496
00:20:59,424 --> 00:21:01,635
Because women really
come into their own as storytellers

497
00:21:01,718 --> 00:21:03,428
when we're waiting on a text back.

498
00:21:04,096 --> 00:21:06,932
[laughter and scattered applause]

499
00:21:07,015 --> 00:21:09,726
Sabrina Carpenter's written,
like, three albums about it.

500
00:21:09,810 --> 00:21:11,144
[laughter]

501
00:21:11,228 --> 00:21:14,481
Casual sex is like religion.
